WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:06.750
The terrorist group ISIS is losing. At the
end of 2014 they claimed a territory the

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:11.280
size of Great Britain and a population
of 11 million people. But today it's lost

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.910
about 60% of that and its population is
down to about two and a half million.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.000
It's the result of fighting its enemies
on all sides.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.940
To the west and north, ISIS is fighting armed rebel groups and the Russian-backed Syrian military and it's allies.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.880
It's also fighting Turkish troops and US-backed Kurdish forces in the north.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.740
And to the east and south, there's the US backed Iraqi army. It was this Iraqi

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.440
army that delivered the latest blow when
they took over Isis's biggest city Mosul.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.780
The group's capital in Iraq. It was a
symbolic loss. Mosul is where the group

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.400
declared a Caliphate, or Islamic
territory, in 2014. This set them apart

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.600
from other terrorist organizations. They
weren't just a network of jihadists

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.360
strung out across several countries like
Al Qaeda. They governed actual territory

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.560
which they called the Islamic state. Now
with the loss of Mosul, the fall of this

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.640
ISIS Caliphate seems imminent. So what
happens when Isis is gone?

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.200
Problem number one is that these fighters are going to want to go home. So in 2014 when

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.520
ISIS captured a huge swath of territory
in Iraq and Syria, tens of thousands of

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.080
foreign fighters came from all over the
world to join the caliphate.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.480
According to UN estimates as of July
2016 there are upwards of 30,000 foreign

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.160
fighters. So most of these fighters came
from countries like Tunisia, Morocco,

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.040
Jordan, Saudi Arabia, but hundreds also
came from countries like Belgium, Germany,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.080
the UK, and even a few dozen from the
United States. So these fighters were

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.960
drawn to ISIS for all sorts of different
reasons, but a big one was that it had

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.280
actual territory and was supposed to be
building a Caliphate based on its own

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.600
extreme interpretation of Islamic law.
ISIS propaganda pushed this narrative in

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.920
flashy violent videos and magazines
online encouraging people from all walks

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.360
of life, not just fighters, to come and
help build this glorious Islamic state.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.080
And the people who came were not just
young men, they weren't just fighters,

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.920
there were women children and even
families who came as well. But now as

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.520
ISIS territory shrinks and the state
that they tried to build collapses,

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.640
anyone who survives will have to find
somewhere else to go... and that's a major

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.160
problem because many of these people are
now indoctrinated, there now

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.320
well-connected and they're capable of
carrying out deadly terror attacks.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.920
A similar dynamic actually happened back in the 1980s in Afghanistan. So thousands

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.920
of foreign fighters flocked to
Afghanistan to help the Afghan mujahideen

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.280
fight the Soviet invasion.
After the Soviets left a lot of these

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.440
fighters dispersed to various different
theaters of jihad including Bosnia,

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.880
Chechnya, Somalia, Sudan, and elsewhere.
These fighters ended up forming a loose

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.440
network of transnational jihadists, most
of whom had links to Osama bin Laden and

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.000
al-Qaeda,
thanks to their time fighting in

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.800
Afghanistan. And some of them actually
went on to carry out attacks against the

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.600
West, including attacks against the
United States. So the worry now is that

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.800
ISIS fighters will do something similar
but this time they might actually make

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.920
it home to places like Europe and launch
attacks there.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.760
So the second problem is what to do with the US military after
ISIS falls. Right now the US is helping

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.160
local forces in Iraq and Syria
to defeat ISIS. There are currently

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.920
around 1,000 US troops in Syria and
around 6,000 US troops in Iraq. Mosul

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.400
which was ISIS's capital in Iraq has
fallen and right now the US is helping

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.920
Syrian local forces to defeat ISIS in
Raqqa its so-called capital there. So as

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.080
those two cities fall ISIS has been
spreading out to rural areas. The Trump

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.840
administration had two choices. Option
one is to keep the US troops there which

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.680
would lead them in harm's way. Or option
two is to bring them home, which

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.680
increases the risk that the region
breaks out into war again. Option 1 is a

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.520
big political risk for Trump. He promised
during the campaign that he would not

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.120
get involved in foreign wars that he
would actually invest American money and

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.920
time and jobs and research in the United
States. And so if Trump were to keep

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.720
troops in the region it would be
admitting that he's going against the

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.760
political philosophy he espoused during
the campaign and currently during his

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.000
presidency. Option two is definitely also
a risk for Trump if US troops were to

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.920
leave that opens a space for sectarian
violence to break out in the region.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.720
Let's take a look for example at what
happened in 2011 the Obama

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.680
administration took most of its troops
out of the region that gave the Shiite

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.760
government in Baghdad the space to crack down on Sunni population throughout the

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.160
country and ISIS took advantage of that.
ISIS was seen as the defender against

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.000
the Shiite government and was able to
gain support and allow for its rise.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
So while it doesn't necessarily mean
that it's troops leave an ISIS type

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.840
group or ISIS 2.0 itself would grow out of
that absence of US troops but that risk

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.040
certainly exists.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:50.480
A deadly California
shooting rampage. A lone gunman to pledge allegiance to ISIS. Both incidents
carried out by so-called lone wolves

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:55.520
Problem number three is ISIS moving from a place to an idea. Up until now ISIS had

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.040
one goal: defend the territory it already
had in Iraq and the territory already

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.000
had in Syria. The problem is on the verge
of losing both. When that happens

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
ISIS goes from being the kind of place
people go to fight to being the idea

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.680
that people fight for.
It's an idea that's compelling it's an

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.960
idea that its followers are willing to
kill for and as followers are willing to

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.480
die for. When they first came to power
people thought it's a group that has its

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.880
own territory, it wants to control that
territory, but it's not al-Qaeda, it's not

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.160
a terror group that wants to carry out
attacks outside of its borders in the

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:32.960
West. That was proven tragically wrong in
October of 2015.. "this is the bomb ISIS

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.040
claims they use to bring down the
Russian passenger plane over the Sinai

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.080
Peninsula." In their online magazine they
posted photos of the bomb that they said

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:45.440
had been used to blow up the plane. And
that was a reminder for was about to

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.440
come. A wave of ISIS attacks that have
killed hundreds of people in London, in

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.800
Paris, and in Nice. And have inspired
attacks that have killed dozens here at

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.120
home in California and in Orlando. It's
worth remembering that these are not

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.640
always attacks carried out by people who
are part of ISIS. In many cases these are

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.360
people who are radicalized in the
countries where they lived and that's

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.040
the danger of the idea. Take what
happened in San Bernardino, California

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.720
where a married couple killed 14 people.
Or in Orlando where a single shooter

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:17.920
killed 49 people. In both cases ISIS
claimed responsibility but in both cases

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.960
there is no connection between those
people and Isis. They got radicalized

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.840
over the Internet
Isis has known that propaganda matters

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.360
and ISIS is really good at it. It has
people all over the world who use English

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.880
language Arabic language to have
Facebook pages and Twitter accounts that

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.400
are full of incredibly slickly produced
videos. They're really compelling and if

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.000
you're someone predisposed to have that
worldview, they can push you over the

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.320
edge from just thinking that's an idea
worth killing for,

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.840
to actually killing for it. ISIS takes it
so seriously

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.040
that it refers to these people as media
operatives and says those media

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.560
operatives are carrying out a form of
martyrdom akin to killing, to literally

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.360
killing for the cause. And at the end of
the day that's the hardest thing about

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.560
ISIS. It can be beaten
on the ground in Iraq and Syria. It is

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.280
being beaten on the ground in Iraq and
Syria, but it's going from being a place

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:09.360
to an idea. And it is impossible,
impossible to defeat an idea.

